  :adr/body
  "## Context

The bots that exist today (4,260 in `manifest/repo-bots.edn`; the imported Hermes
workforce in this app) share no design plane for economic coordination. What
exists is measured and real, and none of it connects bots to each other:

- **Valueflows (REA) is the economic vocabulary of itonami** — ADR-2608153000
  (root, 2026-08-15). `ws-valueflo-vocabulary` carries the 19-action behaviour
  table and `valueflows.event`; `ws-valueflo-algorithms` carries the 8
  network-based algorithms. `scripts/itonami-valueflows-bind.cljs` already
  projects itonami business onto `90-docs/valueflows/itonami-business-vf.datoms.edn`.
  ADR-2608153000's own gaps: cloud-itonami-app Contracts are still not written
  as vf:Agreement; multi-stage recipes never ran.
- **XMILE is the simulation formalism** — `org-oasis-open-xmile` +
  `xmile.execute`, running today in this app's portfolio (`portfolio.clj`
  re-runs per business), `ux_score.clj`, and the root `90-docs/system-dynamics/`
  corpus. ADR-2608153000 fixed the boundary: **VF flow is an economic event,
  XMILE flow is a continuous rate — `:different-formalism`, not convertible,
  and that stays.**
- **Bots have authority but no economic surface.** A workforce Bot's
  capabilities become biscuit scopes `kotoba://cap/<workforce-key>/<cap>` and
  are enforced by `authority.grant/covers?` inside this process. AgentRun /
  Goal gives durable execution. Organism workers already have the
  intent → admission → receipt shape (`organism_gateway.clj`,
  `kotoba.ao.worker-intent.v1`), with `:receipt/capability` and
  `:receipt/effect-status :not-executed` — admission never claims execution.
- The owner asked (2026-09-03): bots に valueflow / xmile を使った連携・同期を
  設計せよ、そして 能力はどう与えるか。

## Decision

**1. The sync plane is shared data, not messages.** Bots do not talk to each
other. A bot that produces an economic fact writes an immutable event to a
shared VF event log; a bot that consumes it reads the same log. Every
cross-bot coordination question becomes one of (a) what event happened,
(b) which commitments does it fulfil or violate, (c) what does the shared
model now say. This is the same split the repo-bots already use — roster is
data, running is state — raised to the economic level.

Concretely: a per-organization VF event journal
(`:vf/event-log`, append-only, one entry per `valueflows.event/apply-event`
shape: agent, action, resource, quantity, time). Bots append through the
existing intent admission path — an intent whose capability is
`vf.event.append` and whose payload validates as a conforming EconomicEvent.
The receipt (`:receipt/status :admitted`, `:receipt/effect-status
:not-executed`) proves admission; the append itself is the effect, and its
own receipt entry records the applied state root, exactly as the
root ADR-2608153000 corpus commits state roots alongside projections.

**2. Agents get capability, not role strings.** Answering the owner's
question directly: a bot's economic authority is a biscuit scope over the
event plane, issued by the existing `bot-authority/issue` root, of the form

    kotoba://vf/<org>/<plane>/<action>

where `<plane>` ∈ `#{event commitment agreement resource}` and `<action>`
carries the VF action verb (produce transfer consume give …). Read of the
shared planes needs `kotoba://vf/<org>/event/read`; append needs the
narrower `.../event/append`; a bot that may promise on another's behalf
holds `.../commitment/commit`. The mapping from loop-yakuwari capability
keywords to these scopes is mechanical and total, in the same way
`capability->scope` is today — a dropped capability is a bot quietly
holding less than its role says, so none are dropped silently.

The decider stays `covers?`. What changes is what the scopes name: economic
planes in the shared journal, not tool invocations in this process. The
biscuit still verifies off-process, still attenuates (`attenuate` exists:
a bot may narrow its own token before handing a slice on), and still never
widens. **Admission is not execution** — an admitted `vf.event.append`
intent still waits for the journal's own effect receipt, and a bot can
never mint events on a plane its biscuit does not cover. A bot holding
`:vf` read-only scopes is exactly the observer bot; observation requires
no grant beyond presence, so most bots should get read-only first.

**3. XMILE is the shared model each bot reads, not a plane bots write.**
Each business already has a model face. The bots' view of 'where are we' is
the current run of that model over the measured inputs, recomputed when the
event journal advances — the same re-run `portfolio.clj` already does per
business, moved behind the journal-advance hook. A bot never writes flow
rates. A bot that measures something appends a VF event; the model's
constants get recalibrated from the journal; the next run reflects it. The
formalism boundary stays: `:different-formalism`, calibration is the only
bridge.

**4. bot 名簿レベル (repo-bots) も同じ面を読む。** The 4,260 repo-resident
bots' charters stay repo-scoped, but their state rows gain a VF projection:
what this repo produced or consumed in economic terms is already in
`90-docs/valueflows/*` datoms; a bot measuring repo health may cite it. No
new bot type is created. The first concrete consumer is `ws-valueflo-*`'s
own bots: algorithm readiness is already a datoms file
(`valueflows/algorithm-readiness.datoms.edn`).

## What this does not do

- It does not create bot-to-bot messaging. If two bots must negotiate,
  each writes a vf:Proposal to the shared journal and reads the other's;
  `valueflows.proposal/reciprocity` and `agreement-balance` are the
  negotiation's arithmetic. Negotiation-in-messages is exactly the shape
  ADR-2608153000's false-friends list warns about.
- It does not make XMILE convertible to VF. The bridge is calibration
  from measured events, one direction only.
- It does not move the run loop on-mesh. ADR-0092's split stands: turns
  stay on the JVM resident until `capability-llm-infer` admits a provider;
  the event journal is durable plane, not execution substrate.

## Capability grant order (concrete, for the workforce as it exists)

1. Read scopes `kotoba://vf/<org>/event/read` (+ commitment/agreement
   read) — all imported workforce bots. Observation first; it is what
   'idle but at work' ADR-2608190400 already assumes a bot can do.
2. `kotoba://vf/<org>/event/append` — bots with a measured production
   surface (commerce tools admitted today: product upsert, shipment
   booking). Their existing tool calls become the sources of EconomicEvents.
3. `kotoba://vf/<org>/commitment/commit` — codinator-class coordinators
   only, and the journal requires the corresponding vf:Agreement to exist
   before such a commitment admits (a commitment without an agreement is
   the false-friend shape).
4. Attenuation is how a coordinator hands a bounded slice to a worker bot:
   `attenuate` with `recipient-did`, scopes narrowed to one org-plane-action.
   The issuer is never consulted; the token cannot widen.

## Verification (this ADR lands as design; each slice gates on its own proof)

- Event journal: an appended event that fails `valueflows.event` conformance
  produces a rejected intent receipt, and the journal state root is
  unchanged. Two implementations (vocabulary's `apply-event` and the
  gateway's admission) agree on the rejection reason.
- Capability mapping: every loop-yakuwari capability keyword maps to
  exactly one scope; `covers?` on the new scopes passes the
  `kotoba://graph/alice*` / `alice-evil` class of test (prefix must not
  cross bot boundaries).
- XMILE bridge: appending a measured event changes exactly the calibrated
  constants named by its resource, and the next model run's inputs match
  the journal — nothing else moves.
- Grant order: a read-only bot attempting append is refused at intent
  admission with `capability-not-granted`, before any journal write.

## Slice 1 landed: the event journal (2026-09-03)

`cloud.itonami.app.vf-journal` (this app, `deps.edn` pins
`io.github.kotoba-lang/ws-valueflo-vocabulary` at the west pin
`4c1e0106`) — per-org append-only EDN journal, gated in fixed order:
conform (`valueflows.conform/conform-event`, bad payload) then fold
(`valueflows.event/apply-event` over the CURRENT inventory, event does not
fit this state), each rejection its own stage; accepted events carry the
post-fold inventory root (sha256) in their own line, and replay CHECKS the
recorded root — a hand-edited line (internally-consistent EDN that is not
what the fold would produce) refuses to load instead of papering over.
Proofs, all measured: conform-reject leaves the journal byte-empty;
conform-pass/fold-reject is a distinct stage; two accepted events fold in
order and the replayed root equals the appended root exactly; the
hand-edited-root tamper is refused. Remaining known failures
(bundle/graph CID lock, bulky-waste HTTP) reproduce identically on the
pre-change base — pre-existing, not this slice's.

## Slice 2 landed: the capability mapping (2026-09-03)

`cloud.itonami.app.vf-authority` — `kotoba://vf/<org>/<plane>/<action>` as
the wire form, with the plane/action table closed (an undefined pair, e.g.
`resource/append`, is refused, never minted). The loop-yakuwari mapping is
three keywords — `:vf.read` → `event/read`, `:vf.append` →
`event/append`, `:vf.commit` → `commitment/commit` — and deliberately
closed with them: a capability outside the set is nil here (it stays a
tool scope under `bot_authority/capability->scope`), because a guessed
scope is invented authority. Decider unchanged: every verdict below is
`authority.scope/covers?` / `covered?` through `authority.grant`, measured
— `org-a` does not cover `org-ab` (segment equality, not prefix);
`event/read` does not reach `event/append` or `commitment/commit` (separate
leaves, the grant order holds as scopes); the decider's `:*` wildcard
covers strictly longer scopes and never the namespace itself;
`attenuate-slices` narrows a coordinator's scopes to the slice it hands on
via `scope/meet`, and an incomparable ask (coordinator holds read, asks
for append) has no meet — nothing is synthesised.

## Slice 3 landed: the XMILE calibration hook (2026-09-03)

`cloud.itonami.app.vf-calibrate` — the bridge stays one-directional and
the `:different-formalism` boundary is not crossed: no flow rate is ever
written, no event is converted into a rate. The correspondence table
(`:vf/xmile`, org → {resource-id → constant name}) is the only place a
resource becomes a model constant. `calibrated-model` rebases each bound
LEAF constant (bare-number equation — the exact shape `loops/sensitivity`
perturbs) to the resource's folded `:onhand-quantity`, and refuses a
binding onto a computed variable (overwriting a derived value would not be
calibration, it would be a different model). `calibration` is the
observer's report: `:rebased` (from → to), `:unmatched` (binding without a
measurement — a stale operating point, the actionable kind of nothing),
`:ignored` (measurement without a leaf-constant place to go). Proof,
measured: a produce of 17 moves ONLY the named constant (28 → 17.0; flow
and aux untouched; the original model unchanged — immutable update); the
next `execute/run` reflects exactly that operating point (final Backlog 20
→ 2.0, the arithmetic of the rebased initial condition); an unnamed
resource leaves the model identical; two appended events land the FOLD's
arithmetic (5+5=10) as the operating point, not the last event. The run
inputs match the journal — nothing else moves.

## Still open

- Intent-path wiring: `vf.event.append` intents admitted by
  `organism_gateway` do not yet drive `vf-journal/append-event!` in the
  gateway's effect phase; the journal and the admission receipts are two
  working halves not yet one loop (the ADR's Verification line about
  agreement of rejection reasons is satisfied at the vocabulary level, not
  yet end-to-end through the gateway).
- The workforce grant rollout (who holds read / append / commit) is an
  operational decision per imported bot, not code.
- `valueflows.proposal` / `agreement-balance` negotiation arithmetic is in
  the vocabulary; nothing in this app reads it yet."}]